/* THE SIBLING func_800BD5C4 IS THE SAME SOURCE with four constants
   substituted -- colour 0xC02020 for 0x802080, mask 0x400 for 0x40, message
   D_800E0E82 for D_800E0E69, and func_80042B68's second argument 2 for 5 --
   and it measures 90 of 90 with the same 2 DIFF, so landing this lands both:
   180 words.

   THE RESIDUE IS ONE COPY, REVERSED. Both words are the message handle
   coming out of func_800990FC. The built form puts it in the long-lived
   pseudo and copies that into the argument register; the target puts it in a
   pseudo that local-alloc gave $a1 -- which makes the argument move a no-op
   and deletes it -- and copies THAT into the callee-saved one. The target
   has TWO pseudos where the built code has one, and the second is assigned
   FROM the first.

   WHAT IS RULED OUT. Every spelling below gives the identical RTL,
   `(set p84 $v0)` then `(set $a1 p84)`, one pseudo, and measures 90/2:

     m = f(); g(h(MSG, m)); k(m);                   the plain form
     g(h(MSG, m = f())); k(m);                      assignment in the argument
     { void *m = f(); ... }                         block scope
     register void *m;                              register storage
     u8 *m with casts at both uses                  other pointer type
     s32 m with casts at both uses                  integer type
     t = f(); m = t; g(h(MSG, t)); k(m);            two locals, copy first
     t = f(); g(h(MSG, t)); m = t; k(m);            two locals, copy last
     t = f(); m = t; g(h(MSG, m)); k(t);            two locals, swapped
     both declaration orders of t and m
     k((g(h(MSG, m = f())), m));                    comma in the argument
     m = f(), g(h(MSG, m)), k(m);                   one comma statement
     k(({ m = f(); g(h(MSG, m)); m; }));            statement expression

   The two-local forms are the interesting ones, and they fail in CSE rather
   than in the allocator: the `.rtl` dump has both pseudos and the
   `(set p85 p84)` copy, and the `.cse` dump has deleted that insn.
   `merge_equiv_classes` (cse.c:849-874, quoted in docs/matching-levers.md
   under "RETURN THROUGH THE PARAMETER to stop cse canonicalising a copy
   away") makes the longer-lived pseudo canonical whenever its last use is
   past the block end, so every use of the short one is rewritten to the long
   one and the copy coalesces away. That lever's own cure -- give the copy's
   SOURCE a use after the destination's last one -- has nowhere to go here:
   the short pseudo's only possible later use is another argument, and adding
   one changes the call.

   WHAT THE LISTING ALREADY CLOSES. csrc/unk_154BC.c:839 is the project's one
   landed instance of this whole idiom, and it does NOT have the two copies --
   but only because its second argument is a call,
   `func_80099194(D_800E1D55, func_80099734(p, msg))`, so the handle never
   reaches an argument register in the first place. That is not available
   here: the block has exactly four `jal`s -- func_800990FC, func_80099194,
   func_80099290, func_800A5720 -- so there is no hidden callee between the
   first two, and the handle really is passed straight through.

   NOT TRIED: routing a third value through the argument that cse cannot
   equate with the handle, and whether the degenerate-loop reference
   weighting can make local-alloc prefer $a1 for the single pseudo the built
   code has. The second is the likelier of the two: the target's shape is
   exactly what one pseudo in $a1 plus one copy would produce, and this is a
   two-word ALLOCATION residue rather than a shape residue, which is the
   class docs/matching-levers.md's `;; N regs to allocate:` entry covers.

   EVERYTHING ELSE MATCHES: the D_800E3D7C identity test and its
   func_8008D330 reset, the 0x9FFFFFFF pointer-range gate, the D_800DDE84
   lookup feeding func_800AD6FC, func_800C4AFC, the 0x4000/mask guard,
   func_80042B68, func_80098B38 and the D_80083460 +0x0A decrement. */

